To count distinct subsequences, we can use dynamic programming. Let dp[i][j] be the number of distinct subsequences of the first i characters of S, where the last character is S[j]. We can compute dp[i][j] by adding dp[i-1][j] (the number of subsequences of the first i-1 characters, where the last character is S[j]) to dp[i-1][k] (the number of subsequences of the first i-1 characters, where the last character is S[k] and S[k] != S[j]). We can initialize dp[0][j] = 1 for all j, since there is exactly one subsequence of the empty string, which is the empty string itself. We can then compute dp[i][j] for all i and j in O(n^2) time, where n is the length of S. The answer is the sum of dp[n][j] for all j, modulo 10^9 + 7.
